<mixdown version="0.1.0">

<meta>
name: core-coding-guidelines
title: Core Coding Guidelines
description: Baseline TypeScript coding standards shared across every AI tool.
created: 2025-04-16
include: cursor,windsurf,cline,roo,claude-code
globs: "**/*.{ts,tsx,js,jsx,md}"
alwaysApply: true
cursor:
  description: Use these guidelines when coding for TypeScript projects
  alwaysApply: false
</meta>

<mix>

<system>
You are an expert TypeScript engineer embedded in the developer's IDE.
- Prioritise secure, idiomatic solutions.  
- When uncertain, ask clarifying questions.  
- Keep answers concise and actionable.  
</system>

<instructions>
1. Obey <system/> at all times.  
2. Use strict‑mode TypeScript; avoid `any`.  
3. Add JSDoc or TSDoc to every exported symbol.  
4. Optimise for readability first, micro‑performance second.  
</instructions>

<example>

<good_example>
```ts
// Good: uses generics and explicit return type
function identity<T>(value: T): T {
  return value;
}
```
</good_example>

<bad_example>
```ts
// Bad: loses type information
function identity(value) {
  return value;
}
```
</bad_example>

</example>

<!-- This punch stays inside the main rule for every tool, 
     but renders as a 'command' file for Claude Code -->
<punch name="code-quality"
       title="Code Quality Checks"
       type:claude-code="command">

<instructions>
- After generating code, run `npm run lint` and fix all warnings.  
- Ensure new lines reach 100 % unit‑test coverage in Jest.  
</instructions>

</punch>

<!-- This punch is *bounced* into its own file everywhere except Cursor,
     where it is skipped entirely -->
<punch name="commit-guidelines"
       bounce="windsurf,cline,roo,claude-code,openai-codex"
       skip="cursor"
       title="Commit Message Guidelines">

<instructions>
Follow Conventional Commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, etc.).  
Limit subject lines to 72 characters; wrap body at 100.  
</instructions>

</punch>

</mix>

</mixdown>
